NOTE ON SCOPE

This MIT license covers the original code in this repository (the platform
glue: display bridge, BLE keyboard host, SD/BIOS loading, build config, etc).

It does NOT cover the third-party MSX emulator core vendored under
lib/fmsx_core/ (Z80, fMSX, EMULib), which is Marat Fayzullin's fMSX/EMULib
and carries its own, more restrictive terms: free for personal/non-commercial
use, not licensed for commercial redistribution. See
third_party_licenses/fmsx_and_emulib.txt for the exact wording, and
third_party_licenses/ generally for the other vendored pieces (C-BIOS,
the ESPlay-fMSX video glue, the BLE_HID_Client pattern) and their own
licenses.

The Macintosh core vendored under lib/umac/ (umac and the Musashi 68000
core) is mostly MIT, but its disc driver comes from Basilisk II and its key
code table from Mini vMac, and those parts are GPLv2. See
third_party_licenses/umac_and_musashi.txt. The freeink-sdk submodule is
its own repository under its own MIT license.

The PC's 8086 core under lib/pc8086/ is M5PaperDOS's, after 8086tiny,
both MIT (third_party_licenses/m5paperdos_8086tiny.txt).

The Commodore 64 core vendored under lib/c64/ is retroelec's T-HMI-C64,
GPLv3 (third_party_licenses/t-hmi-c64_gpl3.txt). A firmware built with it
(every e-ink build) is a combined work under the GPLv3 as far as that core
is concerned, which does not sit well with fMSX's non-commercial terms in
the same binary. For building it for your own device that changes nothing;
for handing binaries to others it is one more reason to clear it first.

The PC 386 in the Android app is Bernhard Schelling's DOSBox Pure, after
DOSBox, vendored under lib/dosbox-pure/, GPLv2 or later
(third_party_licenses/dosbox-pure_gpl2.txt). It is built as a library of
its own and loaded by the app, but it ships in the same APK; the same
caution as for the C64 applies.

The Amiga in the Android app is libretro's PUAE, after UAE and WinUAE,
a git submodule under third_party/libretro-uae, GPLv2
(third_party_licenses/libretro-uae_gpl2.txt), built as a library of its
own. Its IPF disks are read by CAPSImg, the SPS decoder library, a
submodule under third_party/capsimg, whose licence forbids selling it or
using it in anything commercial (third_party_licenses/capsimg_sps.txt).

The MSX2+ in the Android app is libretro's blueMSX, a submodule under
third_party/blueMSX-libretro: blueMSX's own code under a zlib-style
licence, some parts GPL from openMSX (third_party_licenses/bluemsx.txt),
built as a library of its own. The Hotbit stays fMSX's.

In short: this repo is not "all MIT" - only Fabricio Stefani Peruzzo's own
code is. Building this for yourself is fine; selling it or redistributing
it commercially is not, without separately clearing that with Marat
Fayzullin.
